## What happened

Apple removed DSA support from its native cryptographic frameworks (Security.framework / CryptoKit). .NET's `DSA.Create()` on macOS delegates to the platform provider, and starting with .NET 11 it throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** because the underlying OS API is gone.

Linux and Windows still work because OpenSSL and Windows CNG continue to support DSA.

## How to fix it

**Option A — Use the OpenSSL shim on macOS** (least code change):

```csharp
using System.Security.Cryptography;

DSA dsa;
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
    dsa = new DSAOpenSsl();   // requires OpenSSL installed (e.g. via Homebrew)
else
    dsa = DSA.Create();
```

Add a dependency on the `System.Security.Cryptography.OpenSsl` package if it isn't already pulled in by your TFM.

**Option B — Use BouncyCastle (fully managed, no native dependency):**

```csharp
// NuGet: BouncyCastle.Cryptography
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
verifier.Init(forSigning: false, publicKeyParam);
verifier.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
bool valid = verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
```

**Option C — Migrate off DSA entirely** (recommended long-term):

DSA is a legacy algorithm (FIPS 186-4 recommends ≥ 2048-bit keys; most tooling has moved on). If you control both signer and verifier, re-sign with **ECDSA** or **Ed25519** and retire the DSA path.

---

**TL;DR:** macOS no longer provides a DSA implementation. Either target OpenSSL explicitly on macOS, use a managed crypto library, or migrate to a modern algorithm.
